        <p>A Sore ANSW6ll..—Dibba (rather ahort•eightad, overtaking total etranger and slapping him on back front behind): " Rollos, old fellow, how are you? So glad to are you again. Who'd have thought of meet--" Strange,: "Confound you, sir ! How dare you etrike me in that lilmkguenily manner? You ought to be more careful that you've got the right person." Dibbe "Realty, air, I moat apologise, but I took you for the Karl of ; the likeness is mall:, wood ---" Stranger (greatly mollified): " Say no more, air, I mitred. I Tote one how the mistake occurred. Magnificent weather, ien't it? (Moil morning to you, good morning." 01,111 T Gnu, To Bs HIV—An aged 111411 tering across a London street the other day wee rudoly jostled by a youthful bioyelint. The collie. sion inobled the rider off, and the aged gentleman promptly eaught bold of him by the ear. " Con. mond you," he mid, with considerable asperity, " I've a greet ,nind to take you across iny knee and thrash you." Rut he did not do it. He jinn. held the young fellow • minute and then let him go. " Why didn't you thrash hi,o, air?" said • by- stander. " Well," replied the man, " I oertainly would ill hadn't been a Linde afraid that mebby it Iv. • girl."</p>
