        <p>Groat preparations ere being made to give dis- tinction to the Lord 11.3ror's Show by introducing among the old feature with which we are all fami- liar sunie novelties in rocognition of the net that Alderman Daai aa represents Chatham in the House of Commons. There will be side by side on one car models of the Miner's. Isattleehip--of the types of 1837 and 1897. In honour of if r. Sheriff Dewar there is to he a .• "ports cm'," on which will he de- picted cricket, toothed, racing, and other athletics. The four-horse Rocket conch it also to Writ part in the procession. 1 have not hoard yet whether it iv to he accompanieri bre S traction engine or a in her car or a bicycle equal, giving practical ere mplillca- lion of the advances in locomotion.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chamberlain ham not accepted the invitation to too Buddhistl banquet sent Tuesday, and it in understood that he will not be presenton the own- Mon. If Mr. Balfour, skint wipes there in uneer. Minty,. Me, abestni, the tub of replying for the toast of the douse of Commons will devolve upon either Lord George Hamilton or Sir Matthew White Ridley. Ininny event the entire interest seems likely to centre in the speech of the Prime Minis. ter, hut Lord Salisbury is not generally expeeiel to make env startling declaration ot either foreign or borne policy.</p>
        <p>When,in 1859, there was • legel otPcry because the then Lord Chancellor hint made an unknown atuff gownernan a lade, the appointment of Mr.! Justice Blackburn was succe•wfully vindicated on ' the ground that the tole qualification+ regarded had been proved—legal learning WO attainment, That is Jut?. where doubt an-eir in the C.. 1 of Mr. Darling.and probably it it almott unprecedented for a protett to he got up in the form of e petition from the Inn. of Court asking tee Bar Council whether that representative body intends to fri•e expreteion to what in believed to be the general feeling of the whole bee against the recent exordia. of Judicial patronage in the appointment of Mr. Justice Darling. Mr. A•quith'e outepokon condem- nation of the appeintment hart attracted much at- tention, 1111,1 has worm general satiefaction.</p>
        <p>Military men are much idirred by the rumour which c noes from India .bout the want of lunette- to have beet shown by the 2nd Bette- lion of the Roy el Irish Regiment., which in reported and denied, and rep.' rd aid again denied, and explained away by various Irmh rumours. With the mutinous spirit which it raid to hive manifentod itself is alio) coup'e I charge of refusing to march at amities] moment, an if thin were meant to rates feeling of cowardice on the part of Ike batta- lion. But the regiment h. • high record, and a ref meal to march from • feeling 01 Cowardice in the lent thing that any one who known ire hintory will ...ie. At all events,. the Royal Irish hare been ordered back scam the front—at 11111., vveral com- panic, of them —and t in maid that these companion are •utfering esivorely from fever and dysentery. The imputation of the battalion reqiiires that there should he an tweedier., inquiry ordered or state- ment made (rove the Indian heed quarters. It will Ilie• probably he found the, euperstructure of Action hae been roared in In lien eociety on the beele of the nicknom prevailing among the reek and file.</p>
        <p>'columns Sr. naturally proud of the way the tiordoa Highlanders stormed Dargai It must, how- ever, in justice, be noted that of the forty-three killed or injared, fifteen more English, and two husband one was barn in ludic Colonel Mathias himself comes from flavorforderest, in Pembroke- shire, and be and bit unkilted men bare added to the great reputation of the regiment- /at the storm- ing of the 'locum Koth. Ldrknow, nne of the piper. of the Oardos ilighlasdes behaved with greot bravery. Kntering the broach in the front ranks ;of the storming party, be tnpot away inside while 1 tile follow• were at work -miring the enemy oat Piper Findlater, on the :Sikh inst., was not an for- tunate for be wan disabled in both legs stoner, but that did not put him out of action.</p>
        <p>The election at Deptford willron. principally, if net entirely, on the engineers' dinpute It is enti- mated that quits a fourth of the electernt• are in- terested directly or indirectly in engineering and this fact will be readily grasped when it i• remota- that the works of Heaters Humphreys and Tennant are right in the centre of the borough. Such j labourtng population as there is outside of the ea. I gineering trade is thoroughly in •vmpathy with the men, and bee been all along. If. therefore, the !therm candidate can find nympathy with the prin- , ciple involved in the claims of tho men his chance. lof erioniug the spat aboald to good. klr. Arthur Henry Aylmer Morton, th• tionevrentie. clindidete. I will no doubt take inntructions from headqoartern lon thin enter. firerything depond• upon the I Drineldent of the Boast of Trade, to whom the Con- . gortrative central Aloe ere now loolitng to give their candidate each a lead as may win him the popular i Cote.</p>
