        <p>In Mr. William Bent, Deptford has found an ex - cellent nattiest candid.e. He is widely known es a London reformer, one of the most advanced cf pPerrolre.oli er popular ~i,,wbitt, with ,al Id ,Lpi.ber ,r a Ifs;,Smo his resolution to go on devoting himself to outside work. Mr. Benn's appearance in the field, as Mr. Webb'e also would have done, will litt the con- tent entirely away from the wrangle shout CHID&amp; sad who is responsible for the frontier werAnd also from the criticism of Lord Salisbury's policy in the Eastern Moditerranoan. Mr. Bonn says that the light in Cie borough, so Inc as he In c0nc....n0r% will hinge on questionn which come home to the people's doorsteps. By thin ho means that it will be a mag- nified municipal contest. At first eight this woul seem to be a mistake, but in reality heir not far wrong. For nearly every London problem leads straight to groat [moonlit issues, every one oh which will he embodied in the next social pro- gramme of On Liberal party,even as can lm already seen in the outline,. which Front Bench Liberals are beginning to sketch out.</p>
        <p>The London County Council has at present under consideration tbe report of one of the committees, which recommonds that those summoned to servo upOnCoronerA Juries in London should be paid at the rate of two stoningn per attendance. 'fhb, would at not to. total of about £75011 a year. There is • eery general feeling in the Conned in favour of time such payment, as th o duty tells very hoavily upon the I 'wee middle clansint who, by their numbers, form the bulk of the Jurymen, and who can ill afford to leas« their bust..e or lice a day's work. Tee main difficulty in the minds of most of ill. member, seem* to be whether the re- muneration should not he lour or tiny shillings in- stead of two. If the higher scale in carried it will make a coronae* jury summons popular, which it is not at present.</p>
        <p>The group of elections now taking ploce arr likely robe followed by another group before Par- ' bam-nt meet., and a further opportunity will then In. given tor reeving the foeling of the electorate. This second series ore likely to be sawed by the thatgnationa oft too other judges, and by the re- signation of several membera of ['ornament who have had enough of tit. Stephen's. The vacanciee on the bench can ea•Ory be filled up from the rank. of the Ministerial lawyers, •nd as ths favoured ones are expected to be parliamentary members, the *tato of their rvepootier maJontiee, sad rhe safety of the s-st, will determine the choice. The mem- bers who wish to retire from Parliament are for the most port Unionist., but if the (*worriment ran psrssarin them out of their intention this will be done, although an ssveral instances it is Paid not to be possible.</p>
        <p>More deaths ! Sir Rutherford Aleock'e takes the memory hark to the time when Japan • --up to then the most unknown and conservative of nations-- burnt upon the stint ion of Komp.-And when every doseteptton of its quaint custom., de Curtails con- stitutions, it. neenery, and its enuring cities—now SO familiar a theme- came sin startling revelation to the Western World. Within the span of Sir Rutherford'. life there has oe.eurred an unpin- erdentw4 ly speedy throwing riff of the trwdit ions of ages net a lightning-like change to imitation of Weatern ei•ilisstion. involving along with its lone - fits an adoption of. fair chain of I. •iees.</p>
        <p>The other death recall. one of thaw, brilliant (rata of 11rdieh homier* which invest wit h luetre even the hitch-et Inge, of the nttlootl annals. It may be Palely said that Colonel Chard, whose death is now announced, by his pliant defence of Rorke's Owl!. am a lieutenant, accomplialied much beside. keeping the Loins out of Natal. If. r.vi•ed the self-cespect of the English eittion.soriy humilisfed by the down"ue tragedy of module, and he re- stored its pride and confidence in those who fight Its battles.</p>
