        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>Mr Dua Nmus. It to rather Sad to see that bird. of every kind WO so popular a trimming for the newest hats and bonnet.. Certainly a large proportion of thew "ornaments" are what are commonly knows. S. "millinery bird.," things made up of feather. from halfa•donen species, dyed sn unnatural hues, and stuck together to form something more or less reeembling a bird, with the aid of wire, glue, and buckram. Ilia still, too many of our own familiar songsters, stuffed and diatorted until they retain but little of their real beauty, are tined to deck the headgear of tender hearted (7) woman. Apart from this unpleasant feature, the fashion. in millinery prepared for the coining mason am really aensible and pretty.</p>
        <p>Ecnnete are small rnthrr then large, and fit the head clowly, no almost resembling a half hand• kerchief, eo neneple ie their shape. Velvet edged wan fur in the materiel generally favoured, although mine very pretty little bonnets are of jet embroidery on milk winha brim componeil of alter. flare hunches of flowers and velvet ronettes, or feather pompons. klueh more comfortable for winter wear thEn the big picture-hat of last year ie the velvet toque of to-day, with its eoft beefeater crown, and crumpled brim, edged with for or feather trimming, and brightened with touchee of vivid colour. One very clorminv model ix of deep petunia velvet bordered with sable ; the brim in skilfully arranged to form a large Ixor in front, with a clasp of out steel in the centre, end round the crown is a band of steel embroidery, while at the sides are dusters of pink roww, for the old prejudice against blending sum- mer flavvere with fur and velvet ie quite • thing of Om pest.</p>
        <p>•,,, id the very inshionable drenses for present iioniewhat Russian in style, though owing to Li, tight-titling coat prove more becoming than the regular Russian blouse to a woman of, let us son, —ordinary " figure. Green cloth is very suit- able, and this is braided with black, and the collar and raver and cuff's are firstly faced with white cloth, and the braid is laid over this. The convenient fashion of the Russian blouse and separate skirt is being duly taken advantage of for winter wear, as many cloth, serge, and woollen skirts can be worn with a velvet blouse, end • spring or summer gown can be worn out without the risk of lying by and proving unwear. able next *arson. Travelling drewso are slea useful in this style, and it is so rosy to match cloth and serge that the skirt and sleeve., can be sup- plemented by • blouse of the material, much trimmed with braid, or with applique braiding* laid on, and the edges trimmed with for,</p>
        <p>A fashionable blouse is made of beaver cloth in • delicate fawn tint, braided with brown, and has bell sleeves, edged with for, and • fancy metal belt arranged in square medallions over brown bather. A long, eloseAitting jacket of llossan blue Oath, which proves elTe,tual, is adorned with damp, in tubular braid, and h. loose hip Dockets, which give • characteristic style to the</p>
        <p>Nista,. for middle.aged ladies are very cionler in ehape to the dolman of ninny year. back, hut have more width across the arins, and give • cape effect. Viewed from the back, the mantle bee the appearance of • square cape, tied into the waist, and front the side appears to have • long, pointed sleeve. Cloth capes and wrap. are lined with handsome brocades, and much braiding appears on the more elaborate model., or scrolls of wide braid are out- lined by the narrowest brand, arranged in lines which form loope at every inch. The double. breaeted cape, ot the cape fastening to onr. with turned back revere, has Initially a high far and eometimes long revers of for forming • pwleruto. Your loving,</p>
