        <p>Ut ttt.tt•lt ttl .I,•r mot h., wlll ike ..1 Y.,rlt to relinquish 'lir %IS@ t.f ttortttf Was granted by the Q.ll9efl two years roan. Extensive repairo and alterations would have to ne made In fit the vsidence ae a country home, and a nursery Int children ; and the recent sad event given the Duct els the opportunity of spending a part Oi PACII apringohntild .1. 10 desire, at White Lodge, with her rafter. It is ponalble, after ell, that Hushey House mar g lIIP E t press Frectencl, who has been for the la. year or vro desirous nt seeunng an necasional revidence in the Low er.Thames valley, . .</p>
        <p>rhe Ovum. statue of the Emperor Pre.lerick. at Welt- roden, which has lately been any. iled by the Kaiser, la the work of the well-known arultdor Herr Cebu, at Berlin, and the Empreas Froderio.k has aupervised every detail of It. The bright and attractive watering plane, surrounded by the beautiful nations Prime, it. favourite resort of Brit. I, visitors to the Continent, and wan, in the early eighties, the resting place to whi,h the Crown Prince of Germany and hie Engli-, +lto, eel to escape from the narrow etiquette of the bier.... t. . . .</p>
        <p>Every Sunday it -Was their custom to attend evenanng at the little English church (it had not been enlarged at ' that time) at the end of the Ant.gen. N preparations were ever made for the accommodetion of the Imperial visitors, they found their laffusi pew unattended, unlew • zealous churchwarden happened to catch them at the , door. And the msj At, ol the eon, regation were often unaware of their presence, for the Crown Prince in ht. top hat, and frock coat, lo • tywcal Enghsh Irmtie• man, and the Crown Princess was always quietly dressed. It le said of the late Emperor Frederick of Germany, that he disliked intennely to hear anyone speak slight- ingly of women. When he was Crown Prince, an officer once remarked of • wounded c 'wade, that he man " weeping like a woman."—" Never make that com- parison " said the Ihince,with • Irown,—" Cryinr like • !child would he better—women have more fortitude than . .</p>
        <p>--'fb.ere was • time. many years ago. when it was atm- posed that the Q,11,13 and her eldest dameiter were nt saris nee in npinion, on matters domeatic aril political. But the years that have parsed have brnurht Ihanger with them. The Queen'. nio, on many subjects have : became wider, and time^ ,1 the Emmen,. rn,411,.d. , When the old Emperor William died end the Empreas , Frederick succeele.l with her beinve.l himand to the , throne it (lemony. the Udisen sent her thin telegram. ' merely the words Mothei—Sister " They were mar. veilously Tient. And niece thnse toys ,inly the inn! tender 'nye and sympathy have eximie.l ti gown the two sorr.owing svnmen.. . . . . .</p>
        <p>The new Lord Mayor of London exprrweit the desire to entertain at the Guildhall banquet, any surviving wets who were present at the corremponding occaaion in 1837, whow the Queen attended. The announcement Iron hi several responses, but it wins, add Hon Co her Majerty, and the Duke of 17srnbridge, there is only one person now living, of whose presence at the banquet 605 err ago there can be r‘rb pn.giblp,hapht. This is Mrs. Caroline Wheeler, Yellow late hurband was a common Councillor at the tune, and she it now eight,•li re rears of age. Another woman still more diatinguinhod by her long rwanciatinn with the past, is Mrs. M Crea, who is nearly twenty years older, and now in her 103,1 year. She is the wllow of Admiral M'Crea, who fought under Nelson at Trafalgar, and s wreath, rent by her, helped an decorate NeWon's column in L indon, on ,he occason at the recent anniversary celebrettons</p>
        <p>...... Walking and Imuse gowns, coats and can, aro still beau, eatemively braided, and this in the CAW of well- , turned out epecirnens, add, considerably to the expense. , The art of braiding, though is quite may, and those of um with spare time time at nor command, would do well to cultivate it. There are plenty of pattern, to be had, which, laid on any [alma% can he Trickly ironed nfT,and now that broad heard, WWI a well as narrow, the la- bour re considerably diminished. In the course of braidmaking,tlie yarn patter through many procemes. It is first dyed, aml then wound from hanks to spool, then rewound on the braid machine bobbine, and finally adapted to the proper pattern. Most of the machinery la inade in America, or norne coups limn Germany. When the braid leaves the machine, it re ringed no that the !none fibren are burnt off, next the braid le etearned and 1111.41,1thrmigh a heavy calender- lag roller It is then cleaned and picked. Very few factories make both braid, and trimming,. The two tradm am quite diatinct. Solt, light beyond description and dP'i tionaly warm, are the Japanese quilted silk &amp;mining g..iwns and jacket. .no well to the f rejmxnnome, Th,fie first mut leer from I Japan a.me hopelem trimmnut our [ado I, a r.rle I thd market, arid the lab At coesignmenta areas well shaved as the m tst fumy among us could desire. The sleeves are large enough to slip on easily over any amount of clothing, and the colors in whirl, those garments tee made, are as agreeable to the eye as the texture is pled- pant to the touch. . . . .</p>
        <p>Fiv ball gowns bodices rounded at •he shnohlera stead ftquare seem to be moat in favour, while in the matter of sleeves taste incline. to the short under-sleeve of embroidered lace nr net. hancing ersight aod with very alight fulness over the arrns,with soft draperie, or poffings of the moat moderate dimenaions for the opper sleeves, either "[the link., or other material comp ling the dress, or al ribtsin and gams.. Many of Vie ne• French ball gown, poness not even the alight, stop &amp;icy in tin way of sleeves, bat this It a mode ire likely to meet with murk approval on this side the Channel. We still cline to aatin for centre pier., on the dinner tahle, asit I have lately ok,.rnr eery effective speci- mens in light enloured satins, with bitt leaves at the edge, worked in white main and gold thread, the outline of the leaves reating en the tablecloth. Nowadays the vest aim in needlework would seem to be • ennibination of speed end effect, which has been attained In the new cryatal work, with large bold outlined °Clem§ in white silk and gold thread, on linen, la the (' work, • simple leaved outline is rendered rffeetive hy open stitches. forming squares, in some coorse threads of a brilliant color. Thor manufacturers alto-day evident'y understand their public. for they are preparing the needlework it, 1/11101 away that it is more Loan h ill done 1 before it reaches the worker. I In all the boil...holda where tile help nf one maid only</p>
        <p>,I. poarible, the constant knocks and rings of tradesmen . during the early part of the morning is often • terrible hindrance. The running up and down stairt wenn, in. ce•sant. Th. e housekeepers, however, who are N. a.ed with • side entrance can easily get orer this trouble b, writing all orders on • date,and hinging it outside the tor. Each tradesman las he calls should write his name opposite hie order, an , that it may be known the *Alden required hare been i taken down in hi. order book. And it it fa desired to 'peak to nny of them, write" Ring and wait." Many Ihousekeeper. muiradave adopt this plan, with great sue. C 5,01.1 TIC Poommy—Put some scraps of stale bread (shout half • pound) into • barin,iinti allow fhenttoe,ak for •n boor In a pint of cold milk. 'Pim heat up with fork,and remove all lumps. kid • dessertaislonf ill of le...Mated scgar, two winces of chocolate powder, and t wren...beaten separately, the whitry beaten to • • .tiff froth. Bake thin In • buttered pin dish tie Abvit hall an hour.or twenty minute. if the oven is quite hot. Serve iinmedistely iii. taken from the oven.</p>
