        <p>At the Liverpool County Q tarter Sessions, held at the Sessions House, I.iington,Liverpool, on Tuesday, Sir W. Forwmri in the chair, James Quigley (201, lahournr, was charged with at Warrington, on the 23rd 0 lota.r, •• unlawlully and realicioualy did indict opin John Quigley and Norah Quigley (his father and mo•her)cer- lain Orsona bodily harm with • pirsflin pleaded guilty —Mr main, T. Posed (instructed try the 'Town Clerk of Warritivton) appeared to prosecute, and Mated that the indict inent charged the prisoner with baying committed a cruel and ag,ravet,l aaasulr op or his father and mother with a weapon which, tire.. sorry to any, Wl4 omit to frequently owl amonzst peer. pie of an ungovernahle temper in that dia.! [Oct, namely • paraffin lamp. After quarreling With tea father moon, tune • 11•11trat lamp, which burst and injured both his parent.. The husband turned round to attend to his wile's in. farrier., arid whilat he wee .I,•ing no Iha prisoner picked up the brolson part of the lainth the Rl•m h.h.o jagged. and struck hi, father sar..ral hinge on the heed and face, cutting Mr Corrupter Forveroly rho Chairman: You have pleaded guilty ton very brutal and a very urinate.. .1 assault upon your father and mother You might haws lar en brought hal.), in on • +tin more orrino. chargs than the one with which you are now charge I. Yin will haVe to go to rani for ain month, with hard labour. THE SHAN/WEARS: INN AFFRAY. _</p>
        <p>Andrew Traynor (29), lanourer, and Twantas Mc. Name. f 21.1, labourer, were indicted for having at War. rington, on the MO Au,W, Indicted grim..o,a bodily harm upon Thomas Bannon. Mr. Rigby Swift (in- , eructed by Mr. Arthur Browne, appeared to prow- -1 cute, and Mr. linear represented the priwner Traynor. —Io stating the facto of the case, Mr Swift flail the injorier wee, internal, ant were vory grave in lent, and he thought the jury would be able to And that the inj infiwited were not of an ordinary nal ure ; hut that grievous bodily harm had boen c.used. On the evening in question the prnse niter went into the Shaken- pear. Inn public-liner with a man named Nordista, Ile then met McNamee. Somn depute maw, awl Tray- nor, why was atanding near, j iried in the conversation, and when prosecator sunequently weed into the yard the prisoners followed. The prisoner. strut* him, got him down, illtwated and atoned him, knelt up in he cheat, and drolly he herame uneonaciou., and when he awoke to consci none. he fount h•m wit at the Infirm- ary. Evidence in oupport u One statement was celled.— Mr. Seco, on behal, 01 Traynor, contended that hi• client only used hi. flit., not hie feet. It was the other priwner who jurnord on the prowcutor, and ennami,ted the injuries complained rd.—lnspector Spiels., called lay Mr. &amp;Tar, wild he had known Traynor since he wee a , little boy, and he had algae, borne a gond character. , Traynor had not interfered with the prosecution. —By Mr. Swift Tnere, had been tome getting at the wit. nrsiwa. It aardman had la am go, away, end the poli,e did not know where hewn,, Re had been tampered with by someone, but ha did not think 'Traynor had had anything to do wi,h it. —McN %Mee now &amp;warted hit in- nocence. He said it he pleaded guilty he should be tell- ing a he—The jury f.,und that the prieoners were guilty and Sir William Norwood said the Bench had Mendel to commit them for tin month. with hard labour. The as. flank wise a wory rrit,,l on+. ant entirely unprovoked. A WARRINOTON MAXIM' TNEET.</p>
        <p>.. . . Arthur Lon,shaw (32), lahm ..... was charred with having at Warrington, on the 28'h Aug.% 1897, stolen tarn hoots, the property of inrll.4 11•111weli —Mt. J. ' McKeever (instructed hy the Tin Cleek of Warrington) , appeared to prosenYe, and steel the the pro,cdt, sta. engaged in ...eying • customer at his stall on the Saturday night in q aeeirrfl When the prisoner, wild.t hie hack was turned, to.dt ,•tn fr .nt the stall and went swan with thews Halli well Wag MI-waled by some one whn witnereed the theft of sehat the prmoner had done. He followed him and ',lnk the hoots from him The prison." threw the hoots d,wn and butted the prooecutor in the mouth sei'h his head, cutting hit lips and making them Need. The prosecutor held on to him and gown him into custody. There win, counsel added, • previous conviction against the prisoner •—The Chair- man, in naming ...vet., said it was evident th that n prianner wits les.ll,g a life of theft, rind though he had not hror in gad perio.ts he had been in and out for fifteen years fir one kind of offence or another. He would now be committed to gaol for three months with hard labour.</p>
