        <head>MENDELsSOHN'S " ELIJAH."</head>
        <p>MENDELsSOHN'S " ELIJAH."</p>
        <p>Aprop. of the approaching performance of " MOJA " in Parr Hall, Warrington, we publieh the following account of the origin of the text of the oratorio for which we are indebted to Mr. F. Bromley, the conductor of the Warrington Modest Society. "There rim " nays Mr. Crossley, "he no potation that next to Bander,. " Meetuall " rho . Elijah "it the roost popular oratorio in England at the present time. Although ir does not appear that Mendelmohn actually composed any of the music of the work until 1844 Wm year before its Brat production) it in evident that the eubject of the oratorio occupied his mind for several year,. previously. Shortly before his marriage in IfilT he made a novel request for a wielding present to los old mid attached friend Klingemann in London. Mere comes my requeet. Do write for me within the next few weeks the text for a Biblical oratorio, no that I cannot to and compose it during next Summer. Thn last time we piked about it I men- tioned to you two eubjecte which I like equally well 'St. Peter ' or' Elijah.' What I would like hest would be for you to take Elijah ' . . . If you donor care for either of these two eubjecte, then I am willing to take any other—for instance, 'Saul' But somehow I think Elijah and his going up to heaven in the end,would be • most beautiful imblert. . . . Jae think what sort of an</p>
        <p>-- oratorio I ought to ho alai', to write at thin moment, and midi an one rend me. It ought to be your wed- ding premnt to me it would ho the gift t would value omit. Do nor return my request." Kling.- niamin wedding percent did not take the form rug- [Mated by Slendelnashn but shortly afterwards a long correnpondence on the subject took place. [Eventually the any. Schubring came to Mendeln• ltrihn'n weiist noce,but h iru4ol f through- out did a well Ion! of tne nelection and arranging of the text of the oratorio. Ferdinand Hiller ro- ister how one avening going to call on nl endolmsohn he found him deep in the study of the Bible. " Listen 'crl.l latindelenohn. and then he rend in gentle and agitated voice the psniage from the fins book of Rings b.ginning with the word." And be- hold the Lord panned by'" Would not that be splendid for an oratorio?" he exclaimed and three words now turn one of the greats,n choruses in Elijah." The work w.o protocol for the Hist time, with Mendelvishn Hamell conducting, at the Birmingham F-stival in 18441. Tho effoct created by the magnificent mimic was intense, and rho ora- torio which Ivaa thus produced so auspiciounly hen roar since retained ire pspularity and now ranks no one of the groat envision of mown! literature. Fsr many yearn the name of Mr. Charlen Santiey has been asnociatal with the title part in " Elijsh," at all the great Feitival performsnces of the w irk in the country.</p>
