        <p>installation aa Lord Reeron of Glasgow University WWI inultestionably an inter- eating and able discourse. As might have been expected the aubject of the address waslnot of purely scholastic or acadoidc :nature. Mr. Cimenenheis declared with anitable --tholigh not entirely charucteriatic—lmmility that it would Inc preaumptious on his part to follow the example of his predecumors iii the Lord Rectorship and ad- vise the atudents in thu prosecution of the stridiee which are to lit them for their eev.al pram)e in life. Be therefore Teelectod as the theme] of.hia address a mioject in the treatment of which he might feel thoroughly at home, namely, patrio- tism. Mr. ('HAIIIISSLAIN i•, a. everybody now knows, a born mintroversialist. As a debater lie has few Noels, and perhaps no superiors He would, therefore, have boon at • great disadvan- tage compared with some previous Lord !Lectors of the famoits Scotch University if he hail have. limited in the choice of subjeets to question, re- lating, let us say, tin I:reek learning or the "higher matheinatim." lint as a party politician who. , mind is always closely concerne.l with the cants,- I, Tenn. of the hour, patriotism is just one of Chive subjects which might,:under the .rcuinstances, be dealt with freely mid diaterinisly. It would lose none of its charm for Mr. Vii•aseituf tr from the fact that it might be handled in such way as to to give s nil) to io,tntoal opponent. without directly violating any of the noe-party conditions under which Rectorial addrmees are usually delivered. For, after all, even pat- riotism is eminently dutiateable sobject. It depends very largely Ilp.n the way in which you construe the word. Dr. Joimsem was, in his]day, very gmel at donititio., but even ho gave twin very dieerent meanings to the word' patriotism." In his dictionary bogeys that patriotism is " love &amp;MI used for oue'm mitnitry," net then, again, on s certain memorable occasion he roundly declared Milt patriotism is "