        <p>Camases ell over tho kingdom will JbePo.o hoer with sati.faction that the ap- Crest*. peal of NH. Attu Ev• soot Liver- pool, against the decision of the Prescot justice. who fined him for furious riding at Pre•cot July 10th, has been ...awful. It will be remem- bered that Mr. Ei•as was hooked if hi. machine by a constable armed with a crooked stick, and injured rather seriously by hi, violent fall. This action oar/wilily rained it brit case in the intsweste of cyclists everywhere, if IWO!' there was one. At the Liverpool Quarter Sasseins lle Wednesday the appeal, promotial by the Nations Cyclists' Union, so, heewl. The evidence all throng!, was rii ttttt rkable for its conflicting char.. ter, and, wee imagine, there are few indeed who wdi ulisagros with the verdict that the p ilice have more than strateheil a plii their if,. justifica- tion. The officer. calmly &amp;merle.' that the suppl- iant was travelling from 20 to 20 miles nu hour dies Present Hill, hut the examinetion of Con- stable JOHNSON calls for most comment. Ile it was who boldly 110.4101 EYANII fruits the Saddle while going at the speed sworn to, end him ex- planation is that lie put the stick ;miler the czkat's arm for the purpose of running alongside auTrrfradually stoppnig him. Picture tie. °Meer in the accomplishment of this remarkable feat. The evidence called for the appellants.. of • I strikingly indepeoiliint charactur, and on tlm fax* slit bears the stamp of probability. (Lepel:table tradesmen vouch for the fact Hut nine miles an hour was the limit of EVAIISIII,OO,I on the memor- able occasion, and the girl who was alleged by the police to have been in danger if her life owing to appellant's exceptive pace dinned that he wail travelling at such astound at all. After this testi- mony the Hooch were not many minutes in coin- ing ton 10i5.,,. to allow Pm appeal. This van only be looked upon as a direct robed to the over- officioe police, and unintelligent magistrate. of Preload. The po